<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- The Share extension's own manifest, and deliberately shorter than the app's.
     An appex is a separate bundle and Apple reads a separate file for it, so
     copying the app's list here would declare APIs this process never calls —
     which is a false statement in the direction people assume is safe.

     What this target actually is: a staging process. It copies what the user
     shared into the App Group and stops.

     What holds the empty collection claim up is NOT an entitlement. iOS has no
     `com.apple.security.network.client` gate — that is macOS App Sandbox
     reasoning, and on iOS every app and app extension may open a socket
     whatever its `.entitlements` file does or does not name. The claim rests on
     three things `IOSPrivacyManifestTests` reads instead:

       1. this target stages locally and hands nothing off itself;
       2. the one package module it links, `RelayiumShareKit`, is declared in
          `Package.swift` with no dependencies, so no account client, uploader
          or transport is reachable from it even transitively; and
       3. the source of BOTH halves the appex compiles — its own target
          directory and that module — is scanned for `URLSession`,
          `URLRequest`, `NWConnection`, `WebSocket`, `bearerToken`,
          `AccountSession`, `AccountClient` and `CloudClient`.

     The extension's entitlements are still asserted, as a scope-change guard
     rather than a network proof: a keychain group, associated domain or
     background mode appearing there means the staging-only scope widened and
     this manifest needs re-auditing. `IOSSurfaceGuardTests` owns the wider
     source scan, which also bans credentials, key material and logging. -->
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<!-- Empty because this process reaches no network. The email address the
	     containing app collects is collected by the APP, and is declared there. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- The one required-reason API in `RelayiumShareKit`:
		     `SharedDraftStore.stale(_:grace:now:)` reads `.modificationDate` to
		     decide whether an interrupted draft is old enough to sweep. Files
		     inside the app group container, which is DDA9.1.

		     No `UserDefaults` entry: `VerificationPreference` and
		     `SharedDraftInbox` live in `RelayiumAppKit`, which this target does
		     not link. No `SystemBootTime`: `WebRTCLinkTransport` is in
		     `RelayiumKit`, likewise not linked here. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>DDA9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
